{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head e592fcd28c094fd63d4db24706afc1a22c553c76, artifact 8130a34ca147dc9f7cb472015c7507aad99563c8.\n\nEvery condition met, and the interesting ones were met against the live workroom rather than only in tests.\n\nThe charter is stated durably and ratified — f696c18a — and nothing appended before it was. The connector refuses an absent charter, the retired first draft, and any charter that is stale or unratified. That check lives in the connector because the fold does not know what a charter is; it is the connector holding itself to its own contract, and I have said in the documentation that it is detection rather than prevention.\n\nThe connector runs as a separate command holding its own key, submitting through the existing public surface. No kernel or fold change.\n\nAn issue becomes a durable act carrying source, external identifier and the foreign principal as data, resting on the charter and on the clause that admitted it. Verified live: hugh stated clause 0bb7957c, and generalbusiness-ai/gitseq#1 was observed as aa31d08e, signed by github-connector, effective, carrying admitted_by. Because it rests on the clause, retiring that clause would flare the observation.\n\nIdempotency is demonstrated twice over: a unit test that ingests the same issue twice and produces one key, including after an edit, and a live second run that reported \"1 issues read, 0 admitted, nothing new\".\n\nThe connector ingests only what it does not own — ownership by a marker it alone writes, with tests for leading whitespace and for someone quoting the marker in an ordinary comment. The GitHub issues endpoint returns pull requests too, so those are excluded by structure and by test; missing that would have made the inbound half observe the outbound half's own writing.\n\nThe detection-not-prevention limit is stated in the shipped documentation, docs/concepts/connectors.md, not only in the design note.\n\nOne condition I want to be exact about rather than tidy. `make test` did not pass cleanly on the first run: cmd/gitseq-mcp failed TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects at elapsed=3.045s. I verified it is unrelated before dismissing it — this branch touches only cmd/gitseq-github and internal/connector, neither of which cmd/gitseq-mcp imports — and the test passed alone at 2.998s against a bound of about three seconds. That margin is under one percent, which is a defect in the test rather than in this change, and it is filed as f6383a92. Every other package passed, including internal/docset at 117.1s and the new packages. go vet is clean.\n\nThe anchor gate refused my first documentation attempt because the page rested on the charter, which is a propose. It was right: retiring a policy statement says nothing about whether a description of the implementation still holds. The page now rests on artifact 17cb0f7d for the connector code.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8130a34ca147dc9f7cb472015c7507aad99563c8","branch":"request/github-connector-observe","head":"e592fcd28c094fd63d4db24706afc1a22c553c76","status":"ready-for-review","tests":"go vet clean; make test green except cmd/gitseq-mcp TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects, verified unrelated (branch touches only cmd/gitseq-github and internal/connector) and passing alone at 2.998s against a ~3s bound; filed as f6383a92. internal/docset 117.1s"}}